<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · 1849 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Santa Lucia</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A boatman's song from Naples, printed in 1849, inviting people out on the water on a cool evening.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional; Italian text by Teodoro Cottrau</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (some sources credit A. Longo, 1835)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Neapolitan</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Neapolitan</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Naples, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>published 1849</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional Neapolitan song; Italian text by Teodoro Cottrau (1827–1879), published 1849)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Santa Lucia&quot; is a traditional Neapolitan song. Teodoro Cottrau (1827–1879) translated it from Neapolitan into Italian, and the Cottrau firm published it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1849 as a barcarola, a boat song, during the first stage of Italian unification. It was the first Neapolitan song to be given Italian words. Cottrau, often wrongly named as its composer, was the son of the French-born Italian composer and song collector Guillaume Louis Cottrau (1797–1847); various sources credit the music to A. Longo, in 1835.</p><p>The words celebrate Borgo Santa Lucia, a picturesque waterfront district on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Gulf of Naples</a>, in the voice of a boatman inviting passers-by to take a ride in his boat and enjoy the cool of the evening.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> an early edition was published in Baltimore by M. McCaffrey, with an English translation by Thomas Oliphant. In the 20th century the Italian tenor Enrico Caruso made what is perhaps its definitive recording, and Mario Lanza, Sergio Franchi and Elvis Presley, who sang it in his film Viva Las Vegas, were among those who recorded it after him. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> the tune was given new words for Saint Lucy's Day, in the darkest time of the year, and is sung in several Swedish, Norwegian and Danish versions and a Sámi one. It was also sung with Czech words as &quot;Krásná je Neapol&quot;, made famous by Waldemar Matuška, with German words by Austrian student fraternities, and as the tune of the anthem of Silpakorn University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, whose Italian founder, Silpa Bhirasri, was honoured with a Thai translation of the song in 2017.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A boatman's invitation: he praises the evening at Santa Lucia and calls on his listeners to come out in his boat and enjoy the cool air on the water. The mood is gentle and lilting, the rocking of a barcarole.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="2102190" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Enrico-caruso-santa-lucia-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="PLqoNhHYo7Y" data-title="Santa Lucia: Enrico Caruso, tenor (historic recording; the release dates it 1902)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Enrico Caruso, tenor (historic recording; the release dates it 1902)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Enrico Caruso - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PLqoNhHYo7Y" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Luciasången (Sankta Lucia, ljusklara hägring)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new Swedish words to the tune for Saint Lucy's Day</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Natten går tunga fjät</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Swedish words to the tune for Saint Lucy's Day</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Svart senker natten seg</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Norwegian words to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nu bæres lyset frem</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Danish words to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Krásná je Neapol</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Czech words to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Silpakorn Niyom</span><span class="sg-chain-h">anthem of Silpakorn University, on its tune</span></li></ul></section>
